WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.637
Lesson 7, 90 minutes.

00:00:03.092 --> 00:00:05.164
Testing climate evidence.

00:00:05.618 --> 00:00:09.796
This lesson examines climate reconstruction from tree rings.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:15.329
Assigning a ring to a calendar year does not by itself determine that year's temperature or

00:00:15.342 --> 00:00:16.447
rainfall.

00:00:16.908 --> 00:00:22.171
We will define the target climate, build a relationship with instrumental measurements, and

00:00:22.184 --> 00:00:26.559
test whether that relationship predicts observations it has not seen.

00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:31.803
This video contains the explanations for a ninety-minute classroom lesson.

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:35.280
Pause to complete the exercises.

00:00:36.083 --> 00:00:40.137
Relate climate responses to species, site and season.

00:00:40.598 --> 00:00:44.348
Distinguish calibration from independent verification.

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:48.033
Explain the scope and limits of a reconstruction.

00:00:48.493 --> 00:00:53.651
Our calculation uses a simple linear equation, but the main issue is where the equation

00:00:53.664 --> 00:00:54.789
applies.

00:00:55.243 --> 00:01:00.210
A good fit in the period used to estimate its coefficients is only a first step.

00:01:00.664 --> 00:01:05.704
We also need independent testing, biological interpretation, and attention to the

00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:09.028
disturbances discussed in Lesson 6.

00:01:09.831 --> 00:01:13.075
Crossdating assigns calendar years to rings.

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:18.333
Climate reconstruction needs a relationship with a defined climate variable.

00:01:18.794 --> 00:01:22.866
Accurate dates alone do not guarantee accurate temperatures.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.511
Dating establishes which observations belong together in time.

00:01:27.965 --> 00:01:31.235
Interpretation then asks why growth changed.

00:01:31.689 --> 00:01:37.498
A shared narrow ring may reflect climate, but it can also involve ecological stress.

00:01:37.952 --> 00:01:42.110
Keep the evidence for the date separate from the evidence for a particular climate

00:01:42.123 --> 00:01:44.048
interpretation.

00:01:44.851 --> 00:01:48.727
Some sites are temperature limited, others water limited.

00:01:49.182 --> 00:01:52.412
The growth effect of warming differs among sites.

00:01:52.866 --> 00:01:58.695
Consider photosynthesis, respiration, water transport and cambial activity.

00:01:59.156 --> 00:02:03.432
Warmer conditions can extend growth where low temperature is limiting.

00:02:03.893 --> 00:02:08.057
At a dry site, the same warming can increase water stress.

00:02:08.511 --> 00:02:12.965
The sign of a correlation therefore needs a biological explanation.

00:02:13.419 --> 00:02:18.616
Site conditions determine which mechanism is plausible, rather than a universal rule that

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:22.020
wider rings always mean warmer years.

00:02:22.823 --> 00:02:26.515
Choose species and sites sensitive to the target climate.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:31.582
Record age, slope, soils, crown position and history.

00:02:32.036 --> 00:02:35.634
Replicate among trees and across nearby sites.

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:41.398
A tree that is easy to measure is not necessarily a good recorder of the target climate.

00:02:41.852 --> 00:02:46.134
Elevation, soil moisture and competition can all matter.

00:02:46.588 --> 00:02:52.102
Replication helps distinguish a common signal from an individual history, while site records

00:02:52.115 --> 00:02:56.752
explain why neighbouring trees or sites may respond differently.

00:02:57.555 --> 00:03:02.556
Stored resources, foliage and buds can affect next year's growth.

00:03:03.018 --> 00:03:07.143
Previous summer and current spring can act through different pathways.

00:03:07.597 --> 00:03:11.498
Consider biological seasons as well as annual means.

00:03:11.952 --> 00:03:17.413
The year assigned to a ring is not necessarily the only year influencing its growth.

00:03:17.873 --> 00:03:23.169
Conditions in the preceding season can affect stored resources or developing buds.

00:03:23.623 --> 00:03:29.268
Always identify whether a monthly climate variable belongs to the current or previous year.

00:03:29.728 --> 00:03:34.172
This distinction matters when fitting and interpreting a model.

00:03:34.975 --> 00:03:37.923
Ring width measures radial wood increment.

00:03:38.385 --> 00:03:41.977
Density and anatomy can record different processes.

00:03:42.438 --> 00:03:47.023
Stable isotopes reflect physiology and water as well as climate.

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:51.122
Different measurements provide different views of tree function.

00:03:51.583 --> 00:03:56.885
Combining them can help test an interpretation, but adding proxies does not automatically

00:03:56.898 --> 00:03:58.648
improve accuracy.

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:04.543
Check units, formation season and non-climatic influences for each measurement before

00:04:04.556 --> 00:04:07.776
deciding how the records can be combined.

00:04:08.579 --> 00:04:12.310
A fictional example of current-year monthly correlations.

00:04:12.765 --> 00:04:15.259
Read the sign and seasonal pattern.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:19.489
These are not observations from a real species or site.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:26.246
The horizontal axis runs from January to December and the vertical axis shows correlations.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.035
In this fictional example, positive values cluster in spring.

00:04:31.489 --> 00:04:35.200
Selecting the largest value alone is not sufficient.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.884
We need a biological explanation and must account for having searched across many possible

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:42.364
months.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:46.714
Define the season, region and climate variable.

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:51.288
Searching many months or models can select chance correlations.

00:04:51.748 --> 00:04:55.965
Evaluate the selection procedure using independent periods.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:05:02.446
Exploration is useful, but an exploratory result is not independent verification.

00:05:02.906 --> 00:05:07.564
If the test period is used to choose a season or model, it has become part of model

00:05:07.577 --> 00:05:08.715
selection.

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:14.038
Document when each decision was made and reserve genuinely unused observations for

00:05:14.051 --> 00:05:17.024
evaluating the complete procedure.
